1. py/django-xxe — django/core/serializers/xml_serializer.py:195. FALSE POSITIVE. The `pulldom.parse(self.stream, self._make_parser())` call uses `_make_parser()` which returns `DefusedExpatParser`, a hardcoded hardened expat parser (defined at line 408) that disables `feature_external_ges`/`feature_external_pes`, raises `DTDForbidden` on `StartDoctypeDeclHandler`, `EntitiesForbidden` on `EntityDeclHandler`/`UnparsedEntityDeclHandler`, and `ExternalReferenceForbidden` on `ExternalEntityRefHandler`. XXE/billion-laughs are mitigated.

2 & 3. py/django-path-traversal / …-path — django/forms/fields.py:720 (`ImageField.to_python`, `Image.open(file)`). FALSE POSITIVE for path traversal: the flow originates from `ArrayField.to_python` (postgres array form, line 33-34) and geometry field (gis/forms/fields.py:67-73) splitting user string input, through `Field.clean`→`FileField.clean`→`ImageField.to_python`. The value is only ever consumed as in-memory bytes (`BytesIO(data.read())` / `temporary_file_path()` of an already-uploaded `UploadedFile`), never joined into a filesystem path; Pillow's `Image.open` operates on a file object/buffer, and Django's upload handling already sanitizes the upload name. No attacker-controlled filesystem path reaches an open().

4. py/django-archive-extract (4 results). ALL FALSE POSITIVES:
 - django/utils/archive.py:51 `extract()` → `Archive.extract()` → `TarArchive#extract`/`ZipArchive#extract`. Every member name goes through `target_filename()` (lines 138-143) which computes `os.path.abspath(os.path.join(target_path, name))` and rejects anything not prefixed by the absolute target path with `SuspiciousOperation("Archive contains invalid path: ...")` — this is proper zip-slip/tar-slip protection (explicitly covered by tests/utils_tests/test_archive.py:94). `split_leading_dir` also strips leading `/` and `\`.
 - django/utils/archive.py:92 (ZipArchive.extract) — same `target_filename()` guard applies to every namelist entry.
 - django/core/management/templates.py:255 `extract()` — delegates to `archive.extract(filename, tempdir)` i.e. the guarded utility; also only reachable from the `startapp`/`startproject` management command template argument (local operator-supplied CLI input, not remote request data), and extraction target is a fresh `tempfile.mkdtemp()`.
 - django/core/management/templates.py:379 — same guarded `archive.extract` call inside the management-command `extract()` helper.

No new vulnerabilities were discovered during the review. All findings relate to Django's own hardened library code and management-command paths (not remote-user reachable), so none qualify as exploitable true positives.
